  "configuration_id": "config_008",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best (config_005/006, ~0.65 acc) uses scvi_n200 with multiprofile avgpool. We've cleanly isolated that multiprofile aggregation contributes +13pp over single-profile and that transformer vs avgpool is negligible. The one major RNA encoder axis still untested is scgpt. Question: Does scgpt outperform scvi_n200 in the multiprofile setting, i.e., can we push beyond the ~0.65 ceiling by swapping the encoder while holding the proven-best profile/aggregation settings constant? Why this is the best next experiment: config_008 (scgpt, multiprofile, avgpool) changes exactly one variable (RNA encoder) relative to config_005, giving a clean encoder comparison in the highest-performing regime. It avoids the very-weak image modality (0.208 acc) where fusion gains are unlikely, and it avoids redundant aggregation variants (transformer ≈ avgpool). If scgpt matches or beats scvi, it informs which encoder to pair with multimodal fusion later; if it underperforms, it confirms scvi_n200 as the best encoder and redirects budget toward the fusion axis."
}

